    "response": "In short: **no, you probably shouldn't try to make something of the fact that the panel was entirely men**, at least not in terms of explaining why you were passed over or building a grievance. \n\nHere is an analysis of why, along with what is more likely at play and how to handle the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the panel’s gender composition is unlikely to be relevant to your outcome\n* **Both you and the selected candidate are men:** Legally and practically, an all-male panel selecting one man over another does not suggest gender discrimination between the finalists. \n* **Discipline demographics:** Economics historically has had a significant gender imbalance, especially among senior faculty. An all-male panel usually reflects the current composition of the department or committee rather than an intentional selection or hidden agenda.\n* **Institutional policy vs. personal outcome:** While many universities have guidelines recommending or requiring gender diversity on search committees, an all-male panel represents a potential institutional governance or procedural flaw, not evidence of bias against you specifically. Raising this as an unsuccessful male applicant will almost certainly be viewed as grasping at technicalities.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What is more likely behind the decision\n\nWhen candidates reach a shortlist of three, baseline qualifications (having publications, teaching experience, and a PhD) have already been verified. At that final stage, decisions rarely come down to pure numbers (e.g., 6 papers vs. 2 papers). Common differentiators include:\n\n* **Departmental strategic priorities (\"Industry experience\"):** Economics departments—especially those housed in or affiliated with business schools or applied policy programs—often face pressure to hire faculty who can connect students to jobs, teach practical applied tools (e.g., data analytics, financial modeling, consulting), or satisfy professional accreditation standards (like AACSB). A candidate with direct industry background may have fulfilled a specific curriculum or strategic gap the department had identified.\n* **Career stage and perceived trajectory:** In academic hiring, there is sometimes a bias toward candidates who are \"fresh off the market\" (finishing a PhD). Committees sometimes view ABD (all-but-dissertation) candidates as having a clean slate for tenure-track research productivity, or they assume they will be more adaptable to the department’s needs. (While age discrimination does exist, it is notoriously difficult to distinguish from preferences around career stage or publication trajectory without explicit evidence).\n* **The job talk and interview dynamics:** Final hiring decisions often hinge heavily on the job talk, the teaching demonstration, and perceived collegiality/departmental fit during the second interview.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The two-week delay for feedback\n\nTwo weeks without a response to a request for written feedback is completely normal in academia:\n* **Academic bureaucracy is slow:** Offers must be formally accepted, contracts signed, and HR approvals finalized before committees can officially close the search file.\n* **HR liability concerns:** Many universities have strict policies restricting what search committees can put in writing to unsuccessful applicants, fearing legal liability or formal appeals. Often, search chairs are instructed either to give very generic formal statements or to offer informal feedback only via a phone call.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended next steps\n\n1. **Do not raise the panel’s composition:** Raising the all-male panel will not overturn the hiring decision and risks damaging your professional reputation in what is often a small academic subfield.\n2. **Follow up gently for constructive advice, not an explanation:** If you phrased your initial request as demanding an \"explanation of the decision,\" it may have triggered institutional caution. After another week or two, send a brief, courteous note to the search chair:\n   > *\"I understand how busy this time of semester is. If you or any member of the committee ever have 10 minutes for a brief phone call, I would greatly appreciate any constructive feedback you might have on my interview or profile as I prepare for future applications.\"*\n3. **Focus on the signal about industry/applied relevance:** If programs in your region are increasingly valuing industry experience, consider how you can frame your research, consulting, or teaching to highlight applied, real-world applications in your future job talks and application materials.\n",
